  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "n1a",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1000,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "STAGE 1 \u00b7 LANDING AND SIGN IN \u2014 DESKTOP\nRead left to right: the whole first-visit path.\nA stranger arrives, learns what the branch is, joins with four fields, gets an email link, and is in.\nNo password exists anywhere in this stage, so there is no forgot-password flow to design."
    },
    {
      "id": "n1b",
      "x": 1580,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1000,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "Four fields: name, Tech-U email, department, level.\nInstitution is never asked \u2014 a @tech-u.edu.ng address answers it, and the server rejects anything else (2b).\nDepartment and level are buttons rather than dropdowns: two options and five, so showing them is fewer taps than hiding them."
    },
    {
      "id": "n1c",
      "x": 4740,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1000,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "The two screens ending in 'b' are the paths where things go wrong.\nThey are part of the flow, not edge cases: a wrong address and an expired link are the two things\nthat will actually happen, and both are written in plain words with a way forward."
    },
    {
      "id": "n1d",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2740,
      "w": 1000,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "THE SAME STAGE ON A PHONE\nNot the desktop shrunk \u2014 laid out again at 390px.\nThe landing page becomes one column with the four steps on a vertical rail; the level buttons tighten to fit five across;\nCheck-your-email leads with 'Open my mail app', which is the actual next action on a phone and pointless on a desktop."
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1400,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "STAGE 2 \u00b7 MEMBERSHIP \u2014 DESKTOP AND MOBILE\nDesktop on top, the same five screens at 390px below.\nScreen 5 is the new member waiting. Screens 6 and 7 are that same moment from the executive's side.\nScreen 8 is the evening they are approved; screen 9 is the record months later."
    },
    {
      "id": "n2b",
      "x": 1580,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1600,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "Nobody types a matric number, at any point.\nA firstname.lastname@tech-u.edu.ng address already carries the name, so the server matches the applicant\nagainst the department roll and reads the matric number off it. The reviewer sees 'On list' or 'Not found'\nand approves with a glance.\nThis depends entirely on the departments sharing their rolls \u2014 the single highest-leverage ask in the project."
    },
    {
      "id": "n3",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1200,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "STAGE 1b \u00b7 QUICK JOIN \u2014 DESKTOP AND MOBILE\nOne link to drop in a WhatsApp group, or a QR on a flyer at a departmental event.\nSame four fields, no landing page in the way, and it carries who shared it.\nThe mobile pair below is the one that matters: this link will be opened on a phone almost every time."
    },
    {
      "id": "n4",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -210,
      "w": 1200,
      "page": "page-4",
      "text": "LATER STAGES \u2014 MOBILE V1\nEarly phone screens for events, certificates and attendance.\nThey belong to stages we have not worked through yet and will be redone properly, desktop and mobile,\nwhen we get to them."
    },
    {
      "id": "n2c",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1270,
      "w": 1100,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "THE QUEUE IS NOT A TABLE ON A PHONE\nA five-column table cannot survive 390px, so the mobile queue is cards \u2014 and it re-sorts:\nthe one that did NOT match the roll comes first under 'Needs a look', the six that matched follow.\nA summary at the top offers to approve all six together, and the bottom bar makes that one tap.\nSo the executive's job on a phone is: read one applicant properly, approve the rest in a tap."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-type",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -230,
      "w": 1300,
      "page": "page-5",
      "text": "TYPE DIRECTIONS\nSame content, three pairings, so the comparison is honest rather than a specimen sheet of alphabets.\nA is applied across the whole product right now \u2014 say the word and B or C goes in instead; it is a one-command swap.\nEach one carries its own case AND what it costs, because a set where only the favourite gets an argument is a rigged vote."
    }
  ],
